brief:
The directory "invoices/" holds exactly 6 invoice files, plus a note that is not part of the work. Each invoice has a "status:" line that is either "paid" or "unpaid".

Move every invoice into "paid/" or "unpaid/" according to its status line, keeping the file names. Then write "totals.txt" containing:

- one line per paid invoice: the file name without its extension, a single space, then that invoice's total exactly as it appears in the file;
- those lines sorted by total, largest first, with ties broken by file name ascending;
- a final line "total " followed by the sum of the paid totals.

You have 15 tool calls. Listing a directory, reading a file, and moving a file are one call each, so read what you need and no more. When the work is done, reply with plain text.

files.report.js:
// Prints the rows of a CSV on standard input, largest amount first.
const fs = require('node:fs');

const lines = fs.readFileSync(0, 'utf8').split('\n').filter((line) => line.length > 0);
const rows = lines.map((line) => line.split(','));

rows.sort((a, b) => Number(b[1]) - Number(a[1]));

for (const row of rows) {
  console.log(row[0] + ' ' + Number(row[1]).toFixed(2));
}

files.totals.js:
// Prints the total of the amounts in a CSV on standard input, and the largest row.
const fs = require('node:fs');

const lines = fs.readFileSync(0, 'utf8').split('\n');
const rows = lines.slice(1).map((line) => {
  const parts = line.split(',');
  return { name: parts[0], amount: Number(parts[1]) };
});

const total = rows.reduce((sum, row) => sum + row.amount, 0);
const largest = rows.reduce((top, row) => (row.amount > top.amount ? row : top));

console.log(total.toFixed(2));
console.log(largest.name);
